hi everyone my name is urban fossil and  today i'm going to talk about our recent  work ensembles in the robust sparse  model discovery in the low data high  noise limit with active learning and  control this is joint work with nathan  kutz bing branton and steve brunton and  you can find our preprint on the archive  and the code is already implemented in  the latest version of the pi's in the  package all right so easing is an  extension of the cindy algorithm that  tries to robustify  the model discovery especially in the  low data and high noise limit  and in this short video today i will  first review the cindy algorithm and  then talk about our extension and show  some first results  all right so most of you that follow  steve's youtube channel might be  familiar with the cindy algorithm or the  sparse identification of nonlinear  dynamics so the cindy algorithm is a  model discovery algorithm that tries to  identify  models that best fit  non-linear dynamics  purely from data so for example in the  case of the lorenz system we start with  collecting data so we collect a time  series of data  we use this then to compute the time  derivatives  and we then also compute a large library  of candidate non-linear  functions so  functions that we believe could describe  our unknown dynamics of interest  and then after computing these  derivatives time derivatives and this  large library of candidate nonlinear  functions  we solve a sparse regression  problem to find the fewest active terms  in our dynamics and we can do this for  example by using a sequentially  threshold at least squares algorithm  where we  sequentially solve a least squares  problem and then threshold coefficients  that are below a certain sparsification  parameter that we defined beforehand  all right so this is uh cindy now the  challenge with uh sin you're not just  with cindy but with all model uh  discovery uh methods is that  they tend to be very sensitive to noise  and especially in the low data limit so  it's very difficult to find accurate  sparse models if our data is highly  noise corrupted and if we only have very  little data  then a second  when we use cindy we identify a  deterministic model and oftentimes we're  interested in the uncertainty or an  estimate of the uncertainty of our model  coefficients  so what we would like to have is a cindy  algorithm that is  more noise robust  that also  generates or identifies a probabilistic  model and in this direction there are  some recent extensions of cindy that  deal with this so for example in terms  of robustifying cindy the recent  integral formulation or also ensembling  methods were introduced and in terms of  uncertainty uh  some by asian sparse identification  methods were recently introduced  and in our work with e-cindy we unify  and extend  these methods so we use ensembling to uh  robustify cindy and we exploit the  ensemble statistics to produce or  identify probabilistic models  all right now so what do i mean with  ensembling  so instead of using our data and  building or identifying a single cindy  model we can take bootstraps of our time  derivative matrix and  library matrix  and  take these bootstraps and build an  ensemble so multiple cindy models and  with bootstrapping we mean random  sampling with a replacement and apply to  cindy where we have  m samples in time that represent our m  time steps that we measure we would  sample m new rows with replacement from  this original  data and then generate  n bootstraps and identify n cindy models  in the ensemble so instead of just  identifying a single cindy model we  take n bootstraps of our data we build n  cindy models and then we can basically  exploit the ensemble statistics to  find coefficients that are unlikely to  appear in our dynamics we cut them off  and then we aggregate a final e-cindy  model so for example we could then take  the mean or the median of our identified  modal coefficients in the ensemble to  find a final  cindy model or e-cindy model that best  describes our unknown dynamics  and what is really nice about this  method is that we get uncertainty in the  coefficient so we don't just get a  single model but we can  get  model coefficient distributions and also  we get inclusion uh probabilities of our  coefficients so probabilities of how  likely will a certain coefficient appear  in  our  models of the ensembles in the ensemble  of our identified cindy models and what  we can do with this is then to run  ensemble forecasts that provide  confidence intervals of our forecasts so  our e-cindy method connects to sparse  patient interference as we identify  probabilistic models  and the great advantage  of our e-cindy method is that it's  extremely computationally efficient so  compared to for example a markov chain  monte carlo based methods that can  easily take several hours to identify a  cindy model  our  e-cindy  method can identify  probabilistic models within seconds  all right so with e-cindy we can  robustify the cindy algorithms and  identify um probabilistic models that  can be used for ensemble or  probabilistic forecasting  now  in our paper we  apply uh this new e-cindy method to a  range of challenging problems so for  example the first one is where uh we try  to discover an ode uh only based on 21  experimental data points so we take  links in here pelts measurements that  were  measured between 1900 and 1920  and with e-cindy  we are able to identify the  volterra dynamics with only these 21  experimental uh data points so we can  identify  uh models in the very low data limit  then we also apply our e-cinema method  to discover pds and here we show that we  can robustify  the pde model discovery by using uh  ensembl cindy so we show this on a range  of pde data sets and in our paper we  show that by using ensembling our method  only breaks down with twice as much  noise as if we wouldn't use ensembling  so we can clearly show that by using  ensembling we can robustify  the model discovery of pdes  all right  then what is also really interesting and  what we show in our paper is that we can  also exploit the ensemble statistics  for active learning so  often times are actually always it is a  very time consuming and expensive to run  experiments or run simulations  so  we are interested in efficient  exploration so basically trying to  only run experiments or simulation that  maximally inform our model discovery so  we don't want to randomly perform a set  of experiments or simulation but we want  to  efficiently explore our future space to  only run experiments that  maximally inform our model discovery so  that really help us to identify accurate  and sparse models and to do this we can  use e-cindy so we can exploit the  ensemble statistics to find and explore  high uncertainty regions in the future  space so instead of just running a  random set of experiments and building a  single cindy or e-cindy model we can  iteratively identify e-cindy models to  improve the data efficiency of the model  discovery and what i mean with this is  so for example we could start with only  running a very small set of experiments  or simulations and then use this small  data set to build a first e-cindy model  and then we use this e-cindy model to  find out where we have the highest  uncertainty in our future space and we  can do this for example by saying if we  take again the lorenz system dynamics we  use our cindy models and we run a set of  ensemble forecasts starting from  different initial conditions  and then we compute the ensemble of  forecast variance  and  find  the initial condition or the region in  our future space with the highest  uncertainty and then we only sample the  true dynamics at this exact initial  condition so we only  run an experiment that maximally informs  our model discovery  and we show that by using e-cindy for  active learning we can reduce the  variance of our identified model  coefficients and we can improve the data  efficiency of the model discovery uh  algorithm so we can basically find  more accurate models with  less training data or with running less  experiments  all right then the last  example that we show in our paper is  where we apply e-cindy to identify  models that also have external inputs or  control inputs and these models can for  example be used in a model predictive  control framework and in the paper we  show that by using ensembl cindy we can  get more accurate controllers with less  training data  so basically we can get  better control  faster so we don't need to train our  models that long but we can get  very quickly very accurate controllers  by using ecindi  all right so  to summarize easy improves the  robustness and accuracy of cindy  especially in the high noise and low  data limit  easily produces probabilistic models in  seconds  compared to existing variation methods  that can take several hours  and finally we can also exploit the  ensemble statistics to uh  for active learning and for improved  more accurate control  all right so  again you can find our preprint on the  archive and the code implemented in the  latest  version of the piscindi package  thanks for listening
 

